From 02b181f40a019b94299b02b8dc5f334ca74781c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 15:21:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/27] Shaders can draw a smaller buffer than the panel;
 ds-fsr draws 3x on the RG DS Plus

A shader's source may say "// dsflip-output: Nx" (N times the DS screen's 256x192) or "dsflip-output: WxH": its
output buffers are made that size, never larger than the panel, and the display controller (VOP2, bilinear, free)
scales them to the panel, as it scales DraStic's own buffers without a shader. DSFLIP_SHADER_OUTPUT (the same syntax,
or "panel") overrides it. The output buffers are now made by the shader worker once the shader is compiled (under mu:
the buffer generation counter is shared with SDL_CreateTexture). A viewport rectangle (dsflip-viewport) is in the
buffer's pixels and is mapped to the panel.

ds-fsr declares 3x. On the RG DS Plus (1024x768 panels, 4x the DS screen) a panel-sized EASU pass is 2.56x the
RG DS's pixels: at the RG DS's measured 4.8 ms per panel that is ~25 ms per frame for both panels, more than the
16.7 ms refresh. At 768x576 (a 2x game upscaled 2x -> 3x by EASU, FSR's "Quality" ratio, the last 1.33x by the
scaler) it is 56% of that, ~14 ms per frame. On the RG DS (640x480) 3x doesn't fit and nothing changes.

shtest takes OUT=WxH (the panel; a shader's smaller buffer is honoured, as in the library), shbench.sh and shaders.sh
pass OUT through, so the Plus figures can be measured with OUT=1024x768.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01EHHmyacNZtyRhmxZoUYRa8
