From 5e14c95a2cbcd8d9abad95a52703b0ecebb24532 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JorreFog <JorreFog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 18:35:51 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 14/27] DraStic's helper threads wait until their locks exist:
 the freeze at a game's start

DraStic r2.5.2.2 starts four of its helper threads before it initialises what each of them waits on: pthread_create,
then pthread_mutex_init twice and pthread_cond_init twice (at +0x312ac for the thread that starts at +0x2fa50, and at
+0x597b4, three times over, for the three 3D threads that start at +0x58e50). A helper that is quick is already inside
pthread_cond_wait when its creator wipes that condition variable. glibc (2.25 on) keeps a condition variable's waiters
in the variable itself, so the first pthread_cond_signal finds none and wakes nobody, and whoever handed the work over
waits for the answer for good. The game shows no frame, or none from the first frame that needs that helper (a first
3D frame that comes with a state load fits the freeze of 2026-10-05 on an RG DS Plus; no dump exists from that one).

Measured on an RG DS Plus, 0.5.0-beta.1-rocknixds.6: 4 of 25 starts of Pokemon HeartGold showed no frame. gdb: three
with a 3D helper still in the pthread_cond_wait at +0x58eb8 after its hand-off, the 3D thread waiting for it in the
one at +0x592f8 and the main thread in the one at +0x596a8; one with the lines helper in the one at +0x2faf0 and the
main thread waiting for it in the one at +0x30d30. Every thread was allowed on all four CPUs, so it was not the CPU
placement, which .4 took for the cause; placed early it only made the creator late more often.

pthread_create now starts those four through helper_start, where a helper first waits until its creator has made its
second pthread_cond_init since the pthread_create (the last of the four initialisations; pthread_cond_init is wrapped
to count them). A door opens by itself after 2 s. A DraStic whose code is elsewhere is started as before.

DSFLIP_HELPER_DOOR=0 starts them as DraStic does. DSFLIP_HELPER_RACE=<ms> makes the creator sleep that long after each
of the four pthread_create calls. On the RG DS Plus with DSFLIP_HELPER_RACE=20: 4 of 4 starts froze without the door,
0 of 6 with it, and the log has the four helpers waiting 20.6 to 21.5 ms for their locks.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0163Mqir7sNdo9dnTp5gcyfe
---
 docs/INTEGRATION.md |  2 ++
 src/dsflip.c        | 76 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 77 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/docs/INTEGRATION.md b/docs/INTEGRATION.md
index d217cd6..bdd5932 100644
--- a/docs/INTEGRATION.md
+++ b/docs/INTEGRATION.md
@@ -161,6 +161,8 @@ Environment variables, or `superdrastic.conf` beside the launcher (a value there
 | `DSFLIP_STALL_TEST` | off | Seconds after the first frame at which DraStic's main thread stops for good (tests the stall watch) |
 | `DSFLIP_NOTICE` | `/tmp/dsflip-notice` | Where that ending leaves a line for the launcher to show |
 | `DSFLIP_SPREAD_THREADS` | 1 | 0 = new threads inherit their creator's CPUs (with DraStic's main thread confined to one CPU, its new helper threads then share that CPU only) |
+| `DSFLIP_HELPER_DOOR` | 1 | 0 = DraStic's four helper threads start the way DraStic starts them: before the locks they wait on exist. DraStic r2.5.2.2 calls `pthread_create` first and initialises the thread's two mutexes and two condition variables after it; a helper that is already waiting by then never gets its first hand-off, and the game shows no frame (or stops at its first 3D frame). With the door a helper waits for its creator's second `pthread_cond_init`, 2 s at most; the log has a line per helper |
+| `DSFLIP_HELPER_RACE` | off | Milliseconds DraStic's main thread sleeps right after starting each of those helpers. With `DSFLIP_HELPER_DOOR=0` the game then freezes at every start; with the door nothing changes (tests the door on a handheld) |
 | `DSFLIP_GPU_CLOCKS`, `DSFLIP_AUDIO_RATE` | 1, 1 | The launcher's GPU clock and PipeWire rate handling |
