From fce7ed0d5cdbccdca58269b85a758093f1add1c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 18:30:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 20/27] Shaders from the in-game menu: switched while the game
 runs where the session can, kept per game (issues 3, 5)

Quick settings has a Shader row: Off, ROCKNIX's built-ins (when libdrastouch.so is there) and every .frag in the
shader folders. The pick applies as the menu closes:
- In a session that started with a shader, the shader worker compiles the new one between two frames and uses it
  only if it links and draws into buffers of the size this session made (shader_switch); touch follows its viewport.
  "Off" is then a plain copy (the GPU still draws; zero-copy, and its lower GPU clock, come with the next start).
- Otherwise (a zero-copy session, a "dsflip-output:" size that differs, e.g. ds-fsr's 3x on the RG DS Plus) it
  shows from the next start, and a pop-up says so. A shader that doesn't compile is reported and not kept.

Either way it is kept for the game in <DSFLIP_DATA>/menu.cfg ("shader.<game>=<picked> <the frontend's shader>") and
used at the next start (shader_name, through menu_shader_pick) as long as the frontend still asks for the shader it
was picked over: a change of the game's shader in the frontend wins again. superdrastic-run reads the same line, so
the GPU's clock follows the pick. GL still runs on one thread only (the shader worker); the menu waits up to 2 s for
its answer while the game is paused.

shader_init is split into compile_named (source, its dsflip-viewport/dsflip-output lines, compile and link, nothing
global touched) and use_program, which shader_switch shares.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PBVcHtDJUrUv4eL8Vsmzy9
---
